   <p class="eyebrow"><b>05</b> · Choosing the process · HOW-WE-WORK section 1</p>
   <h2 id="s5-h">Pick your level</h2>
-  <p>Every job falls into one of six levels. Find the first one that describes your job. If you are unsure between levels 3 and 4, choose level 3. If you are unsure between any other two, choose the heavier one.</p>
-  <p><strong>Hard limit:</strong> if the work needs more than 5 edits or touches more than one source file (its matching test file does not count), it must go through a written plan: level 3 by default, level 4 only for the large spec-driven builds described in level 4. The one exception is <code>/sc:cleanup</code> and <code>/sc:improve</code> run on a folder (guide 3.7): commit first and review <code>git diff</code> afterwards.</p>
+  <p>The six levels set how much process a change gets. Match the outcome you want first, and any command you named or plan that already exists. Most planned implementation uses level 3; level 4 is only for an explicit or exceptional need for its task-file machinery.</p>
+  <p><strong>Hard limit:</strong> if the work needs more than 5 edits or touches more than one source file (its matching test file does not count), it must go through a written plan: normally level 3, not automatic level 4. The one exception is contained <code>/sc:cleanup</code> and <code>/sc:improve</code> work on a folder (guide 3.7): commit first and review <code>git diff</code> afterwards. Naming a command directly does not waive the written plan.</p>
   <div class="term"><b>Spec:</b> a short written description of what changes, which files, and how you will know it works (guide 3.2).</div>
