-  <p>Level 4 is for building a whole project or major component from scratch, from a spec complex enough that it nearly warrants a PRD and TDD (a product requirements document and a technical design document), when you or your lead chose the extra time and tokens. Level 3 is the default for any planned change, including work that touches many files or takes more than one session.</p>
+  <p>Level 4 is for when you explicitly choose the task-file (MDTM) format, already have an MDTM task to run, or have an exceptionally complex, long-running phased program that needs its research, checklist and phase-reviewer machinery. File count, several sessions, an existing spec, PRD or TDD (a product requirements document or a technical design document), risk or drift concerns alone never make work level 4: a twelve-file feature across several sessions is still level 3.</p>
+  <p>After the level 6 documents, ordinary implementation planning is level 3: <code>/sc:workflow</code>, then <code>/sc:implement</code> when you ask for execution. <code>/roadmap</code> is only for when you explicitly choose it or have the exceptional level 4 need (guide 3.1).</p>
